## Step 2 — Test it

Run this from `~/galaxy`:

```bash
python -c "from collectors.log import LogCollector; c = LogCollector(); a = c.collect(); print('First:', len(a)); print('Cursor:', c.last_cursor); b = c.collect(); print('Second:', len(b))"
```

We're looking for something like:

```text
First: 100
Cursor: s=...;i=...
Second: 0
```

The second result may be greater than zero if new journal entries were generated between the two calls. That's fine.

The important thing is that the **same 100 entries should not be returned again**.

### One important limitation

This cursor is currently **memory-only**.

If Galaxy stops:

```text
Galaxy running
    ↓
cursor remembered
    ↓
Galaxy stops
    ↓
process memory disappears
```

When Galaxy starts again:

```text
last_cursor = None
```

and it will collect the last 100 entries again.

That's okay for this step.

After we verify incremental collection works, the next architectural step will be:

```text
LogCollector
     ↓
cursor
     ↓
SQLite
     ↓
Galaxy restarts
     ↓
restore cursor
     ↓
continue from exactly where it stopped
```

That will make the log collector **restart-safe**.

